clippy changes semantics when replacing unwrap_or_else with unwrap_or_default

### Summary

If the `unwrap_or_else` call contains an invocation to default that specifies a type that cannot otherwise be inferred, then `clippy --fix` will replace it in a way that prevents the inference of the type.

In some cases, this changes the semantics of the program (!), but in most cases just causes a type error because the type cannot be inferred.

### Reproducer

Here is an (admittedly quite silly) minimal reproduction:

```rust
use num_traits::PrimInt;

fn sub_and_box<T: PrimInt>(first: T, second: T) -> Option<Box<T>> {
    first.checked_sub(&second).map(Box::new)
}

fn main() {
    let value = sub_and_box(5, 7).unwrap_or_else(Box::<u64>::default);
    println!("{value}"); // outputs 0
}
```

I expected to see this happen:

```rust
use num_traits::PrimInt;

fn sub_and_box<T: PrimInt>(first: T, second: T) -> Option<Box<T>> {
    first.checked_sub(&second).map(Box::new)
}

fn main() {
    let value: Box<u64> = sub_and_box(5, 7).unwrap_or_default();
    println!("{value}"); // outputs 0
}
```

Instead, this happened:

```rust
use num_traits::PrimInt;

fn sub_and_box<T: PrimInt>(first: T, second: T) -> Option<Box<T>> {
    first.checked_sub(&second).map(Box::new)
}

fn main() {
    let value = sub_and_box(5, 7).unwrap_or_default();
    println!("{value}"); // outputs -2 (!!)
}
```

### Version

```text
rustc 1.70.0 (90c541806 2023-05-31)
